      "eyebrow": "The basics",
      "heading": "Look first. Test after.",
      "html": "<p>The visual inspection checks the installation by eye. No test instruments. No live working. You judge its condition against BS 7671.</p><p>It is not a quick walk round. Regulation 641.1 asks you to spot anything that does not comply.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What to cover",
      "heading": "What to check",
      "html": "<p>IET Guidance Note 3 lists what the visual inspection must cover.</p><p><strong>Switchgear.</strong> Consumer units, boards and main switches. Protective devices present and in good order. Right ratings. Sound terminations. Clear circuit labels. Room to work.</p><p><strong>Wiring.</strong> Cable condition and fixing. Mechanical protection. Safe zones in walls. Containment in good order. Right cable for the place. Any heat damage, such as colour change or burn marks.</p><p><strong>Accessories.</strong> Sockets and switches undamaged and fixed. Right type for the place, such as bathrooms. No bare terminals.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Safety critical",
      "heading": "Earthing, bonding and notices",
      "html": "<p><strong>Earthing and bonding.</strong> Main earth terminal and conductor in place and sound. Main bonding to gas, water and other services. Supplementary bonding where needed. Earth electrode, if any, in good order.</p><p><strong>Notices.</strong> Circuit charts present and right. Periodic inspection notice on the consumer unit. Caution notices on dual supplies. RCD test notice fitted.</p><p>Quick list: consumer unit secure and labelled. Every device present. No heat damage. Cables supported. Earthing and bonding in place. Notices current. Kit easy to reach.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Coding faults",
      "heading": "Common faults",
      "html": "<p><strong>Labels.</strong> Unclear labels are often a C3. If you cannot tell which circuit is which and that is a real danger, it may be a C2.</p><p><strong>Heat damage.</strong> Marks around sockets, melted insulation or scorching in a consumer unit. Expect a C2 or worse.</p><p><strong>No mechanical protection.</strong> Surface cables in a garage or utility room. The code depends on the risk.</p><p><strong>No RCD protection.</strong> Outdoor sockets or bathroom circuits. A C2 where the risk is real.</p><p><strong>Earthing.</strong> Missing main bonding to gas or water is a C2. Hidden wiring you cannot see may need an FI.</p>"
    },
